---
import DocLink from '@/components/DocLink.astro';
/**
 * Getting started: a tutorial with one result, the music player's "Saved to your library" toast (the one of /motion/presence,
 * with a motion of your own instead of a preset). Four numbered steps, the same headings for every framework (the table of
 * contents lists every heading, hidden ones too), each written per framework: install, define a motion, attach it, see it
 * play. The demo is the file of step 3; it imports the module of step 2, motion-toast.ts, which step 2 shows (HTML keeps one
 * file: its module script carries the definition). The toast stays until Undo: an action that leaves on a timer can leave
 * before a visitor reaches it (WCAG 2.2.1). The page ends on what to read next.
 */
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import Code from '@/components/Code.astro';
import GoodToKnow from '@/components/motion/GoodToKnow.astro';
import Fact from '@/components/motion/Fact.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import HtmlExample from '@/components/motion/HtmlExample.astro';
import { MotionGettingStartedReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-getting-started.tsx';
import { MotionGettingStartedAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-getting-started.ts';
import MotionGettingStartedSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-getting-started.svelte';
import { INSTALL } from '@/lib/motion-facts';
import { MOTION_NEEDS } from '@/lib/motion-nav';
// Step 2 shows the module the demos import, as it is (the same file in React, Angular and Svelte).
import definition from '@/examples/react/motion-toast.ts?raw';
import { glossaryHref } from '@/lib/motion-glossary';

// HTML without a bundler: the import map, before the module script of step 3.
const importMap = `<script type="importmap">
  {
    "imports": {
      "@udixio/motion": "/vendor/motion/dist/index.js"
    }
  }
</script>`;

/** What to read next: How it works first, then the two other ways on. */
const pageOf = (slug: string) => {
  const page = MOTION_NEEDS.find((need) => need.slug === slug);
  if (page === undefined) throw new Error(`getting-started: "${slug}" is not a page of MOTION_NEEDS`);
  return page;
};
const next = pageOf('how-it-works');
const then = [pageOf('choosing-an-api'), pageOf('recipes')];

/** A link card of "Next": the neutral container of the site's cards, a lighter one on hover. */
const card =
  'flex h-full flex-col gap-2 rounded-3xl bg-surface-container-low p-5 no-underline transition-colors hover:bg-surface-container-high focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-on-surface';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="getting-started"
  title="Motion: getting started"
  description="A first motion in four steps: install @udixio/motion, define a motion, attach it to a toast and see it play, in HTML, React, Angular or Svelte."
  frameworkSwitch
>
  <Fragment slot="summary">
    In four steps, you install the package, define a motion of your own, and attach it to a toast that enters and leaves
    the page with it.
  </Fragment>

  <p>
    The result is the toast of the music player that the rest of this documentation follows: press Save to library, and
    "Saved to your library" rises into view; it stays until you press Undo, and then leaves.
  </p>

  <h2 id="install">1. Install the package</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <Fragment slot="html">
      <p>Install <code>@udixio/motion</code>, the runtime. HTML needs nothing else.</p>
      <Code data-code={INSTALL.html} data-language="bash" />
      <p>
        No bundler? Serve the <code>dist/</code> folder of the package with your page, and map
        <code>@udixio/motion</code> in an import map, before the module script of step 3. The other files of
        <code>dist/</code> load relative to their own URL: they need no mapping.
      </p>
      <Code data-code={importMap} data-language="html" />
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <p>
        Install <code>@udixio/motion</code>, the runtime, with <code>@udixio/ui-react</code>, which adds
        <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code>. The adapter depends on the runtime, so naming
        <code>@udixio/motion</code> is optional.
      </p>
      <Code data-code={INSTALL.react} data-language="bash" />
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">
      <p>
        Install <code>@udixio/motion</code>, the runtime, with <code>@udixio/ui-angular</code>, which adds the
        <code>udxMotion</code> directive, and <code>@udixio/core</code>. The adapter declares both as peer dependencies, so you
        install them yourself.
      </p>
      <Code data-code={INSTALL.angular} data-language="bash" />
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">
      <p>
        Install <code>@udixio/motion</code>, the runtime, with <code>@udixio/ui-svelte</code>, which adds the
        <code>motion()</code> attachment and the <code>presence</code> transition, and <code>@udixio/core</code>. The adapter
        declares both as peer dependencies, so you install them yourself.
      </p>
      <Code data-code={INSTALL.svelte} data-language="bash" />
    </Fragment>
  </ForFramework>
  <p id="weight">
    What each import path weighs, and when it loads: <DocLink href="/motion/loading-and-weight">Loading and weight</DocLink>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="define-a-motion">2. Define a motion</h2>
  <p>
    A <DocLink href={glossaryHref('motion')}>motion</DocLink> has a name. Define it once with <code>defineMotion()</code>: its
    <DocLink href={glossaryHref('verb')}>verb</DocLink> says when it plays, and its <DocLink href={glossaryHref('keyframes')}>keyframes</DocLink>
    what it moves. <code>appear</code> plays the keyframes when the element enters, and backward when it leaves: the toast
    rises 16&nbsp;px and grows from 96&nbsp;% as it fades in.
  </p>
  <Code data-code={definition} data-language="ts" />
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">
      Put it in the module script of the page, before <code>connectMotion()</code>, as in step 3.
    </p>
    <p slot="react">
      Put it in a module of its own, here <code>motion-toast.ts</code>, and import that module at the top of the file that
      renders the toast, as in step 3: it runs once, before the first render.
    </p>
    <p slot="angular">
      Put it in a module of its own, here <code>motion-toast.ts</code>, and import that module at the top of the component's
      file, as in step 3: it runs once, before the template uses it.
    </p>
    <p slot="svelte">
      Put it in a module of its own, here <code>motion-toast.ts</code>, and import that module in the component's script, as
      in step 3: it runs before the element attaches it.
    </p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    With no <code>timing</code>, the move follows the default
    <DocLink href={glossaryHref('spatial and effects springs')}>spatial spring</DocLink> and the fade the default effects spring, the
    Material 3 ones. To name another, see <DocLink href="/motion/tokens">Tokens</DocLink>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="attach-it">3. Attach it</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">
      Put <code>data-motion="toast"</code> on the toast, with <code>hidden</code>, and call <code>connectMotion()</code>
      once per page: it binds every element with <code>data-motion</code>, now and later. Removing <code>hidden</code> plays
      the entry; setting it plays the exit, then hides the toast.
    </p>
    <p slot="react">
      Render the toast as a <code>&lt;Motion name="toast"&gt;</code>, here in a component of its own, and render that
      component with a <code>key</code> inside a <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code>. When you stop rendering it,
      <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> keeps it until its exit has played. There is no runtime to connect: <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> binds the element it renders.
    </p>
    <p slot="angular">
      Put <code>udxMotion="toast"</code> on the toast, inside an <code>&#64;if</code>, and add <code>Motion</code> to the
      component's <code>imports</code>. When the condition turns false, Angular keeps the toast until its exit has played.
      There is no runtime to connect: the directive binds its element.
    </p>
    <p slot="svelte">
      Attach <code>motion('toast')</code> to the toast, add <code>transition:presence</code>, and put it inside an
      <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code>. When the condition turns false, Svelte keeps the toast until its exit has played. There
      is no runtime to connect: the attachment binds its element.
    </p>
  </ForFramework>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-getting-started" layout="split" previewHeight={208}>
    <HtmlExample slot="html" name="motion-getting-started" />
    <MotionGettingStartedReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionGettingStartedAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionGettingStartedSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">
      The classes are Tailwind classes with the colours of the Udixio theme. With classes of your own, the motion plays
      the same: it does not depend on them.
    </p>
    <p slot="react">
      Save to library and Undo are Udixio Buttons: they need the Udixio theme, set up in
      <DocLink href="/get-started/react">Get started with React</DocLink>. A button of your own works as well: the motion is on the
      toast.
    </p>
    <p slot="angular">
      Save to library and Undo are Udixio Buttons: they need the Udixio theme, set up in
      <DocLink href="/get-started/angular">Get started with Angular</DocLink>. A button of your own works as well: the motion is on the
      toast.
    </p>
    <p slot="svelte">
      Save to library and Undo are Udixio Buttons: they need the Udixio theme, set up in
      <DocLink href="/get-started/introduction">Get started</DocLink>. A button of your own works as well: the motion is on the toast.
    </p>
  </ForFramework>

  <h2 id="see-it-play">4. See it play</h2>
  <p>Press Save to library in the example above, and follow the toast.</p>
  <GoodToKnow>
    <Fact label="It enters">
      <p>The <DocLink href={glossaryHref('entry and exit')}>entry</DocLink> plays the keyframes: the toast rises and fades in. Its live region announces it.</p>
    </Fact>
    <Fact label="It waits">
      <p>
        At the end of the entry the motion lets go of its inline styles, and your classes apply again: the toast is
        <DocLink href={glossaryHref('at rest')}>at rest</DocLink>. Pressing Save again
        does nothing while it is shown. It stays until you press Undo: an action that left on a timer could leave before a
        visitor reaches it.
      </p>
    </Fact>
    <Fact label="It leaves">
      <p><ForFramework as="span">
        <Fragment slot="html">When you press Undo, the exit plays the keyframes backward, then the toast is hidden.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="react">When you press Undo, the exit plays the keyframes backward, then React removes the toast.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="angular">When you press Undo, the exit plays the keyframes backward, then Angular removes the toast.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="svelte">When you press Undo, the exit plays the keyframes backward, then Svelte removes the toast.</Fragment>
      </ForFramework> While it leaves it is <code>inert</code>: it can no longer be clicked.</p>
    </Fact>
    <Fact label="Reduced motion">
      <p>
        Turn on Reduce motion in the site's navigation, and press Save again: the entry and the exit become a short fade. You
        wrote nothing for it. See <DocLink href="/motion/accessibility">Accessibility</DocLink>.
      </p>
    </Fact>
  </GoodToKnow>
  <p>
    That is the whole of it: a name, a verb and keyframes, attached to an element. The other motions of the library, such as
    a list that makes room or a player that grows out of its bar, follow the same steps with another verb.
  </p>

  <section class="not-prose mt-16 border-t border-outline-variant pt-10" aria-labelledby="next">
    <h2 id="next" class="text-headline-small text-on-surface">Next</h2>
    <ul class="mt-6 grid list-none gap-4 p-0 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <li class="sm:col-span-2">
        <DocLink href={next.href} class={card}>
          <span class="text-label-large text-primary">Next: {next.label}</span>
          <span class="text-title-large text-on-surface">What you just did, part by part</span>
          <span class="max-w-[60ch] text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">{next.summary}</span>
        </DocLink>
      </li>
      {then.map((page) => (
        <li>
          <DocLink href={page.href} class={card}>
            <span class="text-title-medium text-on-surface">{page.label}</span>
            <span class="text-body-medium text-on-surface-variant">{page.summary}</span>
          </DocLink>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>
</MotionPage>
